[{:seon.task/name "branches-as-in-production"
  :seon.task/plan "Production is one store and many branches; tests, checks and runs work the same way (DECISIONS: one store, many branches). Today places make their own database: `c/start!` in seon.check and seon.e2e each opens a fresh LMDB store seeded with 2,000 or 300 datoms (~0.45 s each, the open alone 213 ms where an mmap and a schema tx should cost a few ms: find out why), ~170 MB kept per bin/check run; five unit-test files each carried a copy of a fresh in-memory db helper. Correct: bin/check seeds one store per run and each step's root works on its own branch of it (branch! from the seeded commit); a step that proves recovery (durable, window, resume) kills and reopens that same store, as production would; bin/test makes one seeded test store and each test takes a branch of it through one helper (`seon.test-store/test-branch!`). bin/root runs tasks as branches of the one kept store. A separate store is a last resort and says why where it is made. Estimate first: a branch costs about one commit (sub-ms) against ~450 ms per boot; disk per check run is the seed plus each step's own writes. Measure both and put them in the commit."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.check-test/steps-are-branches-of-one-store seon.db-test/a-test-takes-a-branch-of-the-test-store}}
 {:seon.task/name "durable-kills-before-its-agents-flush"
  :seon.task/plan "Reproduction: `durable-write` then `durable-read` on one store, 1 run in 4: durable-read printed `:a3 resumes :dur-clone: [[\"()\" nil]]` and DURABLE FAILED; the clone's committed note was gone. Cause (`seon.check/durable-write`: the clone's `transact!` then `lib/c-kill` of root): an agent's transact! is answered when committed in memory, before the sync that makes it durable (continuous group commit, AGENTS §5), and root's SIGKILL reaches the clone through the fork server's process group before that sync. A write followed by an effect outside the database waits for its flush, and root's SIGKILL of the system is such an effect. Correct: phase 1's writes whose survival phase 2 asserts are made with `{:flush? true}` (or phase 1 awaits each writer's flushed fact) before the kill; a write answered unflushed and lost is the window check's business, not durable's. Deletes the race."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.check-test/durable-asserts-only-flushed-writes}}
 {:seon.task/name "a-run-takes-a-branch-and-deletes-it"
  :seon.task/plan "Plan: notes/archive/PLAN-branches.md (DECISIONS: one store, many branches; branches are light). What exists: `seon.db/delete-main!` (a main and every branch under it, `datahike.api/delete-branch!`), `seon.check/drop-mains` (`bin/check drop MAIN...`, with the mains' agents dirs), `seon.test-store/test-branch!` takes f and deletes its branch, `seon.test-store/test-db` for read-only callers. Correct: a check run, a test and an e2e scenario each take a branch of the one store it is given and delete that branch when it ends (a failed step's branch stays until that step's next run), and never make a store; a check that kills its root proves recovery on its branch of the same store. Proof: after a check step and a test run against a store, its branch set holds nothing they made and no new store exists. Open: (1) lmdb-kill keeps an LMDB env of its own, deleted when green: it runs below Datahike, where no branch exists; (2) done: deleted branches are reclaimed: `seon.db/collect!` runs `datahike.api/gc-storage` through the :sync writer at quiescence when a branch named in main's collected record is gone (docs/storage.md#collection); (3) `seon.source-test` (a generation tree with its own runs/) and `seon.main-test` (what a fresh store holds) still make their own stores, each saying why."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.check-test/a-check-leaves-its-store-as-it-found-it}}
 {:seon.task/name "a-check-run-never-loses-the-seed"
  :seon.task/plan "Reproduction: `bin/check e2e loop` given a relative output path left the store's `:db` branch at 1,282 datoms instead of the seeded ~5,234, and every later `bin/check smoke` on it failed (`n-vars` 42, expected over 360); a fresh store passed. Also `seon.e2e/loop-run` with no directory builds its tree at `/tree` (root's merge run, `:clone 128 /tree`). Correct: find what writes to `:db` (a check may only branch from it); loop-run's directory is required by its contract, so a missing argument is invalid, not run."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.check-test/a-check-run-leaves-the-seed-as-it-found-it}}
 {:seon.task/name "in-agent-never-orphans-its-root"
  :seon.task/plan "`seon.agent-test/in-agent` leaves its root process running when a reply times out (two roots orphaned and killed by hand). Correct: the helper's root ends when the helper does, however its forms answer."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.agent-test/in-agent-ends-its-root}}
 {:seon.task/name "todays-reds-at-their-causes"
  :seon.task/plan "Done. Each red was fixed at its producer: `seon.merge/conflict-value`'s contract (task seon.merge/conflict-value-states-its-input), `seon.check/orphan`'s bound (it read a literal 600000 engine ticks as milliseconds; it now takes :seon.config.agent/test-ticks), the continuous flusher (task seon.writer/durability-is-continuous-group-commit: the windowed `seon.writer/flusher-proc` threw `nil where a java.lang.Number is required` on its notice and main was never synced, failing e2e-loop, e2e-stuck and resume), and `my.schema` registration. The tools-reader red is task jolt.ns/a-build-without-whole-program-inference-compiles-tools-reader. bin/check unsets zsh's BG_NICE, which made its mg/check shards print `nice(5) failed` under a niced caller. Result on that Jolt: `bin/test` 336 tests, 1 failure (seon.db-test/a-moved-store-opens, green on main), `bin/check 100` CHECK OK, no orphaned holder processes."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.merge-test/a-conflict-value-reads-a-pulled-entry seon.procs-test/the-flush-is-continuous-group-commit seon.schema-test/a-registration-counts-the-stored-values-it-refuses}}
 {:seon.task/name "mcp-reads-the-agents-records-once-committed"
  :seon.task/plan "Done: `seon.check/await-records`. bin/check 100's mcp step failed once (:both-recorded-outside false, :agent-records []) and passed alone. Cause: `seon.check/mcp-proof` read the agent's branch (`seon.root/branch-db`) as soon as the MCP client exited, but the agent's record of the outside eval is committed only when the agent is idle (`seon.agent/transact!`), so under load the read came first. Fix, by the harness's one way to wait (events, never a sleep, retry or deadline): tap root's events before the client runs and read the branch again at each of the agent's :commit events (`seon.agent-proc` emits {:type :commit :agent :branch :t}) until its records are there. Reproduce: bin/check mcp-proof beside a full bin/test, red before, green after."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.check-test/records-are-read-at-the-agents-commit}}
 {:seon.task/name "every-report-states-src-against-the-aim"
  :seon.task/plan "`bin/laws growth [BASE]` prints a branch's net src delta and the definitions it added, and docs/merging.md asks every merge's commit to state it, but nothing prints the total or the aim (~10k lines, AGENTS §1), so a merge report carries a delta with nothing to measure it against, and bin/check's verdict carries neither. src was 16,701 lines (wc -l over src/**/*.clj on c3265a8). Correct: `bin/laws growth` also prints one line `src: N lines, aim 10000 (M over)` (N counted as the delta is: git's tracked src files at HEAD), and bin/check's last lines repeat it beside CHECK OK/FAILED. The aim's one home is docs/merging.md (the project's measure, not a Seon runtime bound, so not a config key). Not a gate: growth stays a report, the review reads it. Once `seon.main/drivers-leave-src` merges, N counts the system without its drivers. Proof: a deftest that runs `bin/laws growth HEAD` and reads N equal to the line count of `git ls-files src` at HEAD, and the aim equal to docs/merging.md's."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.check-test/growth-states-src-against-the-aim}}
 {:seon.task/name "every-wait-is-the-event-it-means"
  :seon.task/plan "The harness's waits are root's events, never a poll or a deadline (seon.check's ns doc). What exists: the agent sends {:op :started :id} as its eval begins (`seon.repl/agent-step`) and `seon.agent-proc` emits {:type :started :agent :rid}, awaited before smoke's kill, robust's interrupt and backpressure's flood; the agent sends {:op :tests-started :id :pid} once its test child forks (`seon.agent/run-tests-forked`) and the proc emits {:type :tests-started :agent :pid}, which orphan awaits (no pgrep); the probe proc emits {:type :probe :tag :got} for each message it takes; the render proc tells the web proc ({:op :settled}) when its view has no request in flight, and idle awaits the SSE comment; lifecycle's pause needs no window (Flow's control channel is FIFO). A window that is the measurement (what arrives while paused, nothing answered while held, memory the kernel returns, idle's window) stays, its length :seon.config.check/hold-ms or pages-ms; `seon.check/ping` waits :seon.config.check/ping-ms and a proc that does not answer is recorded :blocked, never read as nil. The windows are named in docs/clocks.md and `bin/laws clocks` reads seon.check and seon.e2e. Open: bin/worktree-check's `timeout 30` goes once the MCP relay answers every id and ends at stdin's EOF (task seon.server/the-server-answers-mcp-itself). Proofs: the proc emits :started and :tests-started; the probe emits what it took; the render and web procs say the view is settled."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/needs #{"seon.check/mcp-reads-the-agents-records-once-committed"}
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.procs-test/an-eval-says-it-started seon.procs-test/a-test-run-says-its-child-forked seon.check-test/the-probe-says-what-it-took seon.procs-test/a-tab-hears-its-view-settled}}
 {:seon.task/name "a-build-rebuilds-what-its-pin-moved"
  :seon.task/plan "Done in bin/build: `build_seon` calls every step it needs unconditionally, and each step decides by its own stamp (target/NAME.stamp holding the source pin: the submodule's commit or a digest of its sources; AGENTS §5, a build's result keyed by its sources). Replaced: `[ -x $JOLT ] || build_jolt` and `[ -f lib ] || build_lmdb|lz4|zstd`, existence checks that, after a sync moved the Jolt pin, built target/seon on the old target/jolt (every root-booting test errored `No such var: jolt.host/runtime-fingerprint` until a full `bin/build`). Proof (measured, in the commit): after a pin move, `bin/build seon` rebuilds Jolt and the root-booting tests pass with no other build; with the pin unchanged, every step says up to date."
  :seon.task/kind :work}
 {:seon.task/name "idle-opens-its-window-after-the-fork-server-caught-up"
  :seon.task/plan "Done: `seon.check/fork-server-caught-up`. Reproduction: in a full bin/check 100, idle failed with :steps-at-idle {:api [18 21] :fork-server [4 7]} in its 5 s window, green alone. Cause: main's commits reach the fork server process (`seon.fork-server/server-step` :tx: d/with plus `seon.program/load-changes!`), which answers each with :applied; under load those answers arrive inside the window, one step of the :fork-server proc and one of the api each. Late real work, not an idle defect. Fix: before the window idle sends {:op :stats} through the fork server (`seon.root/fs-call!`), a FIFO barrier whose answer follows every earlier commit's :applied; checked, not assumed: the answer's :t must have reached main's t read before the request, else :fork-server-caught-up fails. The window still fails on any step at all. Proof: idle beside a full bin/test, red before, green after."
  :seon.task/kind :work}
 {:seon.task/name "a-test-root-names-its-store-and-main"
  :seon.task/plan "A test that starts a root inherited SEON_STORE and SEON_MAIN from its caller (a bin/check run's), so its root opened a shared store whose main another run had written, and the test failed far from its cause (bin/worktree --at's exit 128). Correct, one helper so this class cannot come back: seon.test-store/start-root! {:store :main :runs :args} launches bin/root start through binary-sh with SEON_STORE, SEON_MAIN and SEON_RUNS named from its arguments over the caller's (store and main required by its contract, never defaulted) and its server port -1; `ask` writes one request line through bin/ask and answers the reply as data, or one recorded value, {:error :eof|:reset|:refused|:no-answer}; `stop-root!` answers {:exit :log} or an error value; `kill-root!` is answered once the root's whole process tree has ended; `next-root!` restarts it on its next generation. Every root-starting test goes through it (seon.root-test, seon.agent-test, seon.source-test). The server port is the one the root bound: :seon.config.root/server-port -1 asks the kernel for a free port (0 stays no server, >0 that port), the server prints the port it bound on its ROOT LISTENING line, and bin/root records it in root.port; a port found by ServerSocket 0 and closed is free for another process to take under load. bin/root's waits are events: its holder reads the root's output until ROOT LISTENING or EOF (the root's death), and stop and kill read the root's exit status, and for kill that the whole tree ended, from fifos the holder writes. What a check or a test starts ends when its starter ends (8 `seon lease` roots outlived the bin/check that started them by hours): the starter holds a fifo open read-write while it lives (bin/check: fd 7, closed in every child; a test process: seon.test-store's, which Jolt's sh/sh children do not inherit) and names it in SEON_STARTER_FIFO, and at its EOF the child's process group is SIGKILLed, the agents' mechanism, outside Seon's process (a root forks its fork server only while it is the one active Chez thread). One holder does all of it, bin/held, which replaced bin/root's inline perl holder: its fork, stdin pipe and reaping, plus options (--detach --drain --log --pids --ready --done). bin/check runs every ./seon step under it, binary-sh every command a test runs, bin/root every root; --drain makes held outlive its command until every process the command started has ended (a lifeline pipe every descendant inherits), with no clock. Proofs: (a) each moved test green with another store and main exported to bin/test (red before for root_test, whose main came from SEON_MAIN); (b) seon.check-test/every-test-root-starts-through-start-root!: in the program db of test/, no var but seon.test-store/root-sh refers to binary-sh with a root launch in its form; (c) bin/check lease killed mid-run leaves no root of its own, and a test runner killed with a test root up leaves none."
  :seon.task/kind :work}
 {:seon.task/name "idle-waits-for-the-flush"
  :seon.task/plan "Done: the writer's {:op :clean} (`seon.writer`), `seon.root/store-clean!`, and idle's :flush-covered-main check. Reproduction: a full bin/check 100 at load 10 failed idle with the api and the flusher each stepped once in its window (IDLE OK alone). Cause, as for the fork server (task idle-opens-its-window-after-the-fork-server-caught-up): late work, the group flush of main's last commit (`seon.writer/flusher-proc` syncs on {:op :dirty}, answers {:op :flushed :commits n}, and the writer's flush notice steps the api). Fix: the writer answers {:op :clean} once its store is clean (at once if it is, else in the step that takes the covering flush), after that flush's notice to the api; idle awaits it before the fork-server barrier and checks it: :flush-covered-main only when the answer's commit count has reached the count the writer held before the request. A store with no durability answers at once. Proof: seon.procs-test/the-writer-answers-clean-once-the-flush-covers-its-commits red before, green after."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.procs-test/the-writer-answers-clean-once-the-flush-covers-its-commits}}
 {:seon.task/name "one-watch-waiter"
  :seon.task/plan "What exists: two watch-waiters for the same job. `seon.check/await-ref` (check.clj:102, any reference) and `seon.await/await-db` (e2e.clj:20, main's connection) each add a watch, check once and deref a promise; await-ref's docstring calls itself await-db's mechanism. Audit row A17's remnant: the verdict printer and eval-records twins are gone. Correct: one waiter, `await-db` defined over await-ref (or the reverse), in the harness. It moves with `seon.main/drivers-leave-src`. Deletes: ~8 lines."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.check-test/lease-ends-with-a-verdict}}
 {:seon.task/name "attach-answers-when-its-agent-is-ready"
  :seon.task/plan "Root, 2026-10-23, from task a-test-root-names-its-store-and-main: seon.agent-test/in-agent asks the server {:op :agents} up to 300 times 0.2 s apart until the attached agent is idle, a poll (AGENTS section 9). Cause: {:op :attach} answers when the run is started, before its agent is ready, and no request waits for readiness. Correct, at the server (seon.server, the attach function): the attach answers once its agent is ready (its :ready event on root's events, tapped before the spawn, as seon.check's ready! does), or the answer carries what to await; in-agent then sends its forms after the attach answer, with no loop. An agent that ends before it is ready answers that end (a recorded verdict). Proof: in-agent with no wait loop, green beside a full bin/test; an attach whose agent dies before ready answers the death."
  :seon.task/kind :work}
 {:seon.task/name "a-root-stops-on-a-signal-not-on-stdin"
  :seon.task/plan "Root's review of jolt/test-root, 2026-10-23: seon.check/root-serve loops (read-line) until its stdin closes, and bin/root stop ends a root by closing that pipe (bin/held's TERM closes the root's stdin): stdin as a lifecycle, AGENTS section 9's mistake, with one cause for two ends (a stop and a vanished holder). Correct: the root ends on a signal (its shutdown hooks already stop it on SIGTERM, SIGINT and SIGHUP: seon.check/root-serve's end!), bin/held sends TERM to the root's process (group) at its stop, the holder keeps no pipe to the root's stdin, and root-serve parks on the stop's promise instead of reading stdin (bin/mcp's relay and the owner's `bin/root stop` unchanged). Proof: bin/root stop on a root idle for 5 s ends it cleanly (ROOT STOPPED, exit 0) with the root's stdin /dev/null; a SIGKILLed holder ends it through the starter's fifo. Needs bin/held on main."
  :seon.task/kind :work}
 {:seon.task/name "a-hand-started-root-has-a-starter-or-says-it-stands"
  :seon.task/plan "Root's review of jolt/test-root, 2026-10-23: a root started without SEON_STARTER_FIFO does not die with its starter (the owner's standing root is meant to outlive the shell that started it; a root a worktree session starts by hand from its tool shell is not). bin/check and every test root always name a starter fifo (bin/held; seon.test-store/root-sh; the one-launcher check proves no test launches a root another way), so what remains is the hand start. Correct, a decision before code: either `bin/root start` requires its caller to say which it is (a standing root: --standing; any other names the starter, or is refused with the fifo it needs), or a root started from a session's tool shell inherits that session's starter. Evidence to gather first: how many `bin/root start` calls in session transcripts were never followed by a stop, and what each held (the 8 `seon lease` roots of 2026-10-23 were bin/check's, now covered). Proof: the chosen form refuses or ends an unnamed, unstanding root."
  :seon.task/kind :work}
 {:seon.task/name "a-killed-starter-leaves-no-fifo"
  :seon.task/plan "A starter's fifo (bin/check's check-starter-<pid>.fifo, a test process's test-starter-<uuid>.fifo) piled up in target/tmp: 21 in one worktree, and 3 after one ordinary test run, one per shard. Cause: bin/check removed its fifo in a zsh EXIT trap, which zsh does not run on TERM, and a test process removed nothing. Chosen, of three: (3) a fifo named by its starter's pid, removed by the starter when it ends or is terminated (bin/check: EXIT and TERM, INT, HUP traps; a test process: a shutdown hook), and reclaimed by the next starter when its pid is no process (`bin/held --reclaim DIR`, kill -0: a fact about a process, not a clock; a reused pid keeps a stale file and never removes a live one's). It adds no mechanism beyond that option on the one holder. Not chosen: (1) a fifo per held launch, unlinked by its watcher once open, which would leave zero files even after SIGKILL, because it needs the starter to hold one write end per launch: bin/root start returns while the detached root lives, so start-root! would keep that fd until stop-root!, and bin/check's background shards would inherit each other's write ends and never see EOF unless every child closed every launch's fd; (2) an inherited descriptor, which Jolt's sh/sh children do not get (measured: 0 open). The limit that stays: SIGKILL runs no handler, so a SIGKILLed starter leaves its one fifo until the next starter's reclaim. Proof: seon.check-test/a-starter-that-is-gone-leaves-no-fifo-after-the-next-starter (a dead pid's fifos go, a live pid's and an unrelated name stay); by hand, a test process and a bin/check run leave 0 fifos after a normal end and after TERM, 1 after SIGKILL, 0 after the next run."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.check-test/a-starter-that-is-gone-leaves-no-fifo-after-the-next-starter}}
 {:seon.task/name "root-stop-ends-on-its-holders-end"
  :seon.task/plan "Root, 2026-10-23: the first bin/update after test-root hung 10 minutes in `bin/root stop`. The standing root had been started by the old bin/root (an inline holder with no --done), so it stopped cleanly but nobody wrote the done fifo, and stop's `read -u 3` on a fifo it had opened read-write itself never ended: a wait with no end and no fact, whenever the holder is gone or did not write. Correct: the wait ends on the holder's end. bin/held keeps its --done fifo open for its whole life (bin/root start now makes it at launch), so while the holder lives the fifo has a writer; `bin/held --end SIG PID FIFO N` opens the reader first, sends the signal, and prints the first N lines (the command's exit status, then \"drained\") or ends when the last writer closes, the holder's exit. A holder that is gone has no writer, so it answers at once with no status (\"exit unknown\"); a path that is no fifo (a holder from before this channel) is refused at once and stop tells the root to stop without waiting (a one-time transition for roots started before it). bin/root stop and kill use it. Proof: seon.check-test/a-stop-ends-on-its-holders-end-and-a-gone-holder-answers-at-once (a live holder answers its status, a SIGKILLed holder answers at once, a non-fifo path is refused with status 2; a hang is the failure); by hand, bin/root stop prints exit 0 in 0.8 s, kill prints drained in 0.8 s, and the old wait still blocks after 3 s on a fifo nobody writes."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.check-test/a-stop-ends-on-its-holders-end-and-a-gone-holder-answers-at-once}}
 {:seon.task/name "a-run-builds-the-binary-only-when-something-reaches-it"
  :seon.task/plan "Root's test-time measurement (2026-10-23, not kept): on jolt/process-tests bin/prove took 176 s cold, of which 46 s was `bin/build seon` that no selected test or step used, and ~6 s of bin/check --steps seeding and dropping branches for laws and lmdb-kill, which use no store. Cause: bin/test built target/seon whenever any shard or step ran (\"a test that starts ./seon runs the binary\"), and bin/check --steps seeded, listed steps through the binary (`./seon steps`) and dropped every step's main before and after, whatever it ran. Correct: build only when a selected test or step reaches the binary. seon.program/reach-index answers :binary? (a reached form references a var of :seon.config.source/binary-vars, seon.test-store/binary-sh, the one way a test runs target/seon), narrower than :process? (a shell such as git is a process, not the binary); seon.proofs/step-needs-binary? (a step with functions in check-steps runs a seon.check mode; laws and lmdb-kill do not) comes from the one list; the plan writes out/binary (the selected keys that reach the binary) and out/steps-binary; bin/test builds only if out/binary is not empty; bin/check --steps seeds, lists, stores and drops only if out/steps-binary is not empty (it asks no binary which steps exist: the plan took the names from check-steps); and a step that runs no mode is stored green whatever tree the binary was built from. Saves ~46 s plus ~6 s of a cold bin/prove that selects only binary-free keys. Proofs: seon.source-test/a-test-that-reaches-a-process-is-found-by-the-reach-walk (:binary?) and seon.proofs-test/a-check-step-is-keyed-by-what-its-function-reaches (the predicate); in the REPL, the plan of an unnamed run, of a name that reaches no process, of a name that only shells out, and of a root test: out/binary empty for the first three, naming the root tests for the last."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.source-test/a-test-that-reaches-a-process-is-found-by-the-reach-walk seon.proofs-test/a-check-step-is-keyed-by-what-its-function-reaches}}
 {:seon.task/name "a-checks-roots-take-explicit-config"
  :seon.task/plan "Found 2026-10-23 by the merge session: bin/check robust FAILED in the main tree, and passes in a clean checkout at the same HEAD (worktree session store ran it green three times). The cause: every process reads its checkout's config/local.edn as a layer whenever the file exists (seon.config/load-layers). The main tree's local.edn is the standing root's: its :seon.config.source/root names the write-back tree outside the checkout, and its ports are the standing root's. So a check's root in the main tree, and the fork server it restarts, imported the standing root's source instead of the check's program. After the fork server was killed, the next agent counted 2614 :var/name where the check's store holds 360, and load errors named code from that tree (seon.root's \"No such namespace: spi\"). A check's or a test's roots take their config explicitly (the shipped file, then their own --config and --set); config/local.edn is the standing root's layer, given by bin/root (and what reads its ports) with --config, never read implicitly. Write set: seon.config/load-layers (local.edn no longer an implicit layer), bin/root (names it for a root without a starter) and bin/ports (for seon ports), .gitignore (bin/ports' temp file, which a shard's git add raced with), docs/config.md. Proofs: config-test's layer test, red then green; and bin/check robust in a worktree whose local.edn names the write-back tree outside it, red before (lag 8941 ms, the spi load error) and green after."
  :seon.task/kind :work
